COMMAND NAMES, ACCESS AND THE ASTERISK

CALLER. Subject to the current caller access policy.

A - Purpose, syntax and examples

:A
COMMAND NAMES, ACCESS AND THE ASTERISK

CALLER. Subject to the current caller access policy.

Syntax:
    HELP COMMANDS

An asterisk (*) marks a SYSOP ONLY command or section. Those entries
document maintenance tools; reading help does not grant permission to
execute them. Caller commands remain subject to the current BYE
read/write/execute policy.

Public disk programs are in A0:. Private utilities are in SYSUTLS: (I13:);
hidden startup files and A15: backends are also private. Resident CCP/RCP
entries marked * require wheel. Resident flow commands do not bypass those
restrictions.

Names can overlap. GET is the resident memory loader; SYSUTLS:GET
redirects console input. R resets disk state; SYSUTLS:RESET resets the
machine. Public TYPE differs from wheel-protected resident TYPE. Public
DIR differs from SYSUTLS:CPMDIR. IF is resident flow control; SYSUTLS:IF
supplies extended tests. SYSUTLS:NAME edits a directory name; typing NAME:
selects a directory.

DU: and NAME: selection syntax does not need a separate command page. The
reference covers installed named command programs and resident package
entries, not every program that might exist inside an optional mounted
collection.

See HELP COMMANDS for the command index.
